PreserveJxl vs zenjpeg-recompress JPEG — RD at matched quality (2026-05-28)
==========================================================================

12 real source JPEGs (seed 11, product-images + codec-corpus). Three methods
swept and compared at matched SSIMULACRA2 (all decoded via jxl-oxide against
a per-file consistent lossless reference = our lossless transcode decoded by
jxl-oxide — eliminates cross-decoder bias):
  - zenjpeg_JPEG:  zenjpeg-recompress recompress() output JPEG (the baseline).
  - pipeline_JXL:  zenjpeg JPEG transcoded losslessly to JXL (same coeffs).
  - PreserveJxl:   jxl-encoder coefficient-domain coarsen -> YCbCr JXL.

Per-file Pareto lower-envelope interpolation to common ssim2 targets, summed
over files reachable by ALL methods (n_common).

Result — PreserveJxl is the smallest at EVERY quality level:
  ssim2 88: 79.0% of zenjpeg-JPEG  (-21%)
  ssim2 85: 78.5%                  (-21.5%)
  ssim2 82: 79.3%                  (-20.7%)
  ssim2 79: 76.1%                  (-23.9%)
  ssim2 76: 73.1%                  (-26.9%)
  ssim2 73: 69.9%                  (-30.1%)
PreserveJxl also beats the zenjpeg->transcode pipeline by 2-5pp (coarsening
directly to JXL skips the JPEG-Huffman re-quant intermediate).

Conclusion: lossy JPEG->JXL recompression (PreserveJxl) is ~21-30% smaller
than the best JPEG->JPEG recompressor at identical perceptual quality. This
is the core "better than zenjpeg" result. Self-contained in jxl-encoder.

Caveats / next:
- Metric is ssim2; the compendium recommends calibrating final knobs on cvvdp
  (closer to JOD). The relative win holds (both measured on the same metric).
- n=12 real-world sources; win is large + monotone across all levels.
- The uniform-scale knob is RD-lumpy (round-to-zero cliff at the +/-1-AC
  scale); a deadzone / target-quality-calibrated knob is the next refinement
  for smooth quality targeting (won't change the Pareto front, improves
  controllability).

Repro: benchmarks/jpeg_lossy_rd_compare_2026-05-28.py

--- Deadzone update (2026-05-28) ---
Added AC deadzone widening (coarsen_coefficients_dz) + no-size-regression
guard. The deadzone drops the perceptually-cheap ±1-AC residue (the JPEG's
own quantization noise) gradually instead of all-at-once, fixing the
uniform-scale cliff. On anton, scale 1.5 + dz 0.2 = 51,018 B at ssim2 83
vs 105,634 B (no dz) at the same ssim2 — HALF the size.

Re-run of the matched-quality RD comparison with a (scale × dz) grid
(benchmarks/jpeg_lossy_rd_compare_dz_*.tsv): PreserveJxl now 67-80% of
zenjpeg-recompress JPEG, biggest gains mid-range (ssim2 79: 76.1% -> 71.6%):
  ssim2 88: 79.0%   ssim2 85: 77.2%   ssim2 82: 80.0%
  ssim2 79: 71.6%   ssim2 76: 70.1%   ssim2 73: 67.4%   ssim2 70: 69.9%
No-size-regression guard: encode_jpeg_recompress_codestream returns the
lossless transcode whenever coarsening doesn't shrink it (never a larger,
degraded file). 4 unit tests lock: scale<=1 no-op, dequant-value preserved,
deadzone zeros small AC but never DC, shared chroma table scaled once
(share-scale bug guard).
